{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-71123",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ (-t)^{2}+2(t)^{2}+6(t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
